# SOUND (memory safety only) — the `&` spelling of the same rebind must not
# corrupt the heap.
#
# ⚠ THIS FIXTURE PINS MEMORY SAFETY, NOT THE RATIFIED VALUE. The ratified
# semantics of `for x in &coll` are WRITE-THROUGH, so the language-correct
# stdout here is `zz` / `zz`. It prints `aa` / `bb`: the write is LOST. That
# residual is filed as `todo/t1404` and is NOT this fixture's subject — the
# `&`-rebind write is dropped at EVERY element type on EVERY lane (`int`,
# `Vector[int]`, struct and String alike), so it is a pre-existing universal
# class, not something this fix created or widened.
#
# What this fixture DOES pin: the same `Vector[String]` shape used to SIGABRT
# with a double free (rc 134) under the `&` spelling exactly as under the bare
# one — the sigil was never the discriminator. On the severity ladder
# (mem-unsafety > silent-wrong-output) moving this cell out of heap corruption
# and into the already-filed lost-write class is a strict improvement, and the
# lost write stays visible in `t1404` and in the `#[ignore]`d
# `attack_99_amp_for_in_element_assign_double_free`, which still asserts the
# ratified `zz` / `zz`.
#
# PINS the `insts.rs` view half. RED-verified against the pre-fix compiler:
# rc 134 double free.
void main():
    Vector[String] v = Vector[String]()
    v.push("aa")
    v.push("bb")
    for s in &v:
        s = "zz"
    for s in v:
        print(s)
